description:
pkix-lint/src/deviation.rs:738-744 implements hex_encode as:

```rust
fn hex_encode(bytes: &[u8]) -> String {
    let mut s = String::with_capacity(bytes.len() * 2);
    for b in bytes {
        s.push_str(&format!("{b:02x}"));
    }
    s
}
```

Each `push_str(&format!(...))` allocates a fresh 2-byte String, formats into it, copies the bytes into `s`, and drops the temporary. That is one alloc + one copy per input byte on top of the `String::with_capacity` pre-allocation that was supposed to be the optimisation.

The sister implementation in pkix-lint/src/oscal/emit.rs:822-837 already does it the cheap way:

```rust
fn hex(bytes: &[u8]) -> String {
    let mut s = String::with_capacity(bytes.len() * 2);
    for b in bytes {
        s.push(hex_digit(b >> 4));
        s.push(hex_digit(b & 0x0f));
    }
    s
}
```

No per-byte allocations. Either:
* Replace deviation.rs hex_encode with the emit.rs hex shape, OR
* Use `write!(&mut s, "{b:02x}").unwrap()` which avoids the intermediate String allocation (`write!` writes directly into the target).

This is the canonical 'Vec::with_capacity then immediately allocate inside the loop' anti-pattern from the clippy subsection of the prompt. Note: PKIX-7f92.14 already calls out that the three hex routines should be consolidated; that finding is the structural fix. This finding is the local-correctness fix for the deviation.rs site even before consolidation.
